          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Also known as glycogenosis—rare inherited disorders of defective or deficient enzyme activity governing glycogen metabolism.",
              "pdf_page": 655,
              "printed_page": 573,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Tissue glycogen accumulation—leads to organ enlargement and dysfunction; may affect liver, heart, skeletal muscle, kidney, and",
              "pdf_page": 655,
              "printed_page": 573,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "CNS.",
              "pdf_page": 655,
              "printed_page": 573,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Impaired hepatic glycogenolysis— leads to symptomatic hypoglycemia (neuro",
              "pdf_page": 655,
              "printed_page": 573,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "glycopenia).",
              "pdf_page": 655,
              "printed_page": 573,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Classification—based on enzymatic defect and primary organ(s) involvement: more than 12 types in humans, 4 types in dogs (Ia, II, III, and VII), 1 type in cats (IV).",
              "pdf_page": 655,
              "printed_page": 573,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
